	abstract = {Traditional monolithic systems struggle to scale effectively in the current global e-commerce landscape, characterized by high transaction volumes and unpredictable traffic spikes, and require real-time responsiveness. This paper conducts a systematic review of High-Throughput Event-Driven Architectures (EDAs) as the base design approach for developing flexible and responsive fulfillment systems with low latency. The research investigates core principles, including asynchronous communication, service decoupling, and event-carried state transfer, alongside advanced design patterns such as Event Sourcing, Saga orchestration, and Command Query Responsibility Segregation (CQRS). Real-world case studies from Walmart, eBay, and Shopify demonstrate how EDA can enable sub-second order processing, dynamic inventory synchronization, and rapid elasticity. The review in this paper also evaluates cloud-native event brokers, serverless frameworks, and event meshes for their ability to support global and distributed deployments at scale. Additionally, the paper includes a section specific to implementation challenges faced while implementing EDAs in real-world scenarios and the resolution of those challenges through architectural best practices, which include idempotent consumers, versioned event contracts, and Dead Letter Queues for distributed debugging, schema evolution, and event ordering. The review establishes EDA as a strategic business enabler that delivers speed, fault tolerance, and competitive agility for e-commerce fulfillment operations. Towards the end, the paper identifies three future research areas, including adaptive event routing and observability in federated systems, as well as the integration of AI-driven decision-making within event streams. © 2025 IEEE.},
