	abstract = {ETC (Ephemeral Trust Computing) is a credential-minimizing authorization architecture that avoids storing long-lived authenticators at the protected resource server (RS). An issuer mints a uniformly random 256-bit bearer session identifier with a 300 s TTL and stores a short-lived, MAC-authenticated session record in a registry. A request is authorized only if RS (i) retrieves the record, (ii) verifies an issuer HMAC over authenticated record fields, (iii) validates request bindings (w,r,a) and expiry, and (iv) atomically consumes the record, enforcing accept-at-most-once semantics. The design distinguishes cryptographic assumptions (SHA-256, HMAC, TLS) from operational assumptions (registry atomicity/consistency, bounded clock skew, and issuer-side policy enforcement at the AS–ISS boundary). We implement ETC as containerized microservices with pluggable registry backends (in-memory, Redis, PostgreSQL) and a fail-closed verifier. In a contention audit with up to 32 concurrent requests per session identifier (300 total runs across backends and concurrency levels), Redis and PostgreSQL achieve strict single-use (exactly one acceptance per run). In a networked critical-path experiment (fresh session per trial), mean issuance+store+authorize latency is 13.5 ms (Redis) versus 16.5 ms (PostgreSQL) in our measured environment. In a 3-instance RS deployment behind a round-robin load balancer, we observe stable mean authorization latency of 1.17 ms and aggregate throughput up to 23.8k req/s under 100 concurrent clients in our synthetic harness (local deployment; single-machine testbed). We quantify credential minimization as reducing long-lived secret material at RS from Θ(N) per-principal secrets in common deployments to Θ(1) verifier secrets (with an explicit blast-radius discussion), and we benchmark ETC against JWT, JWT+revocation, OAuth2 introspection, and server sessions under identical harness assumptions. © 2026},
	author_keywords = {Atomic consume; Bearer tokens; Credential minimization; MAC-authenticated records; One-time sessions; Replay prevention; Session registry},
	keywords = {Aggregates; Authentication; Cryptography; Memory architecture; Network security; Trusted computing; Atomic consume; Bearer token; Credential minimization; MAC-authenticated record; Minimisation; One-time session; PostgreSQL; Replay prevention; Session registry; Trust computing; Routers},
	type = {Article},
