	url = {https://www.scopus.com/pages/publications/105051068160?origin=resultslist},
	abstract = {Root cause localization is vital for operators to quickly identify the triggering service for mitigation, and it is often defined as ranking candidate services for an incident. In practice, anomalies usually propagate along call chains, several services may become abnormal within a short period, and observations from metrics, logs, and traces can be noisy or incomplete, which makes service-level ranking unstable. To address these issues, we propose a lightweight approach that aligns multi-modal data and derives a training-free deviation signal from normal data. We then define three indicators, residual energy, first-hit time, and cross-modal consistency, and fuse them via rank-based normalization to produce the final service ranking. To evaluate TORCL, we conduct experiments on two public benchmarks. Results show that our method achieves a higher Avg@5 than representative baselines with a low time cost. Ablation results further indicate that the three indicators contribute complementary benefits, and combining them yields the most stable performance across fault types. © 2026 IEEE.},
