	abstract = {Creating a new architecting paradigm is intended, typically, to enhance specific aspects of the existing paradigms. Nevertheless, the initial immature architecting manner of any novel paradigm leads to the appearance of some challenges, which require remedial strategies or components. To illustrate, the microservice architecture emerged to encourage autonomy and Separation of Concerns. On the other hand, many issues accompanied this architecture including client applications and microservices communication, user authentication and authorization, service discovery, and data consistency. Accordingly, researchers and practitioners worked over a decade to come out with solutions that minimize the negative impact to preserve the architecture's provided benefits. This paper first investigates the emerging problems of the microservice architecture to draw their relationship with the architecting pillars of the architecture. After that, it observes the evolution of the architecture over the years including the related challenges and solutions. The purpose is to propose a comprehensive design that combines the best remedial components and mechanisms. That is based on the advocation level of researchers and practitioners in the literature. The analysis of the microservice architecture throughout the paper showed that extreme decentralization and component fine-graining are the two essential causes of the architecture issues. Moreover, the inspection of the remedial solutions demonstrated that API gateways, message brokers, authentication and authorization microservices, and service registry components are essential for any microservice-based system. © 2026 Author(s).},
	type = {Conference paper},
	publication_stage = {Final},
	source = {Scopus},
