	url = {https://www.scopus.com/pages/publications/105053301593?origin=resultslist},
	abstract = {When implementing authorization policies across microservices, their individual development and decentralized evolution introduce consistency errors that slip through static analysis and compartmentalized testing. They manifest as runtime security vulnerabilities leaving no trace until exploited. Identifying such authorization consistency errors demands complex in-depth analysis of interservice communication pathways and intra-service call graphs, which is infeasible to perform manually at scale. Existing approaches lack the ability to automatically verify end-to-end authorization consistency across distributed service boundaries. To guarantee consistent authorization policy while reducing developer burden, we apply a formal method-based approach that provides an automated solution. Our methodology encodes microservice system source code into a system constraint model that enables fully automated formal verification of authorization policy across the entire system without requiring manual specification of system behaviors. Formal reasoning and proofs demonstrate the correctness of the methodology. A proof of concept is developed and applied in a case study, which shows high precision and computational performance in identifying authorization policy inconsistencies. Our formal proofs and case study establish the correctness and practical applicability of our methodology for identifying and addressing consistency errors. © The Author(s), under exclusive licence to Springer Science+Business Media, LLC, part of Springer Nature 2026.},
