	url = {https://www.scopus.com/pages/publications/105037442410?origin=resultslist},
	abstract = {The food industry has always been characterized by traceability issues, resulting in information inconsistencies along the supply chain, poor interoperability between the stakeholders involved and systems not capable of providing data in real time. A more robust and decentralized framework is required to address these drawbacks and to ensure agri-food data consistency and integrity “from farm to fork.” This work presents a novel blockchain-based framework for decentralized and tamper-proof traceability in the dairy sector. The architecture is built on a permissioned blockchain platform - based on Hyperledger Fabric - with two organizations: farmers and producers. Customized JavaScript Smart Contracts - known as “chaincodes” in the Hyperledger environment - are deployed on the blockchain to manage all required activities along the supply chain. Personalized chaincodes define the logic and the rules for a specific microservice in the platform (e.g., user management and batch tracking). Data exchange among parties is possible and, more importantly, secure, by the TLS cryptographic protocol. Each stage of the dairy supply chain is recorded immutably on the shared ledger, together with sensitive data (such as temperature and humidity) provided by smart sensors. A lightweight Web Interface that allows only authorized users to interact with the blockchain has been designed for a more user-friendly dairy supply chain traceability platform. In contrast with traditional systems, this approach proposes a hybrid on/off-chain food traceability framework designed to enable decentralized traceability across stages of the supply chain, allowing actors to invoke identity-based transactions with controlled access. The presented project is extensible and adaptable in other domains, providing a feasible architecture for next-generation food traceability systems. This strategy strengthens the existing technological and management aspects and innovates the control of the food supply chain, giving the concrete possibility to stakeholders involved to cooperate while preserving transparency, security and interoperability within a single infrastructure. © 2025 IEEE.},
