	abstract = {Migrating a legacy monolithic application to a microservice architecture is a complex, multi-stage process requiring careful service boundary identification and an orchestrated transition plan. This paper presents a comprehensive AI- accelerated methodology for domainification - the systematic alignment of microservices with business domains - and for building configuration-first microservice platforms that preserve system flexibility. Modern AI techniques automate and optimize each migration phase: (1) Graph-based code analysis using node embeddings and Graph Neural Networks (GNNs) uncovers tightly coupled functionality clusters, (2) Natural Language Processing (NLP) on source code (identifiers, comments, documentation) identifies semantic groupings and candidate boundaries, and (3) Reinforcement Learning (RL) formulates extraction sequencing as a decision problem, recommending an optimal incremental order that maximizes cohesion and minimizes risk. Crucially, each service is implemented as a configurable engine: business rules and policies are externalized to configuration files or metadata, enabling new requirements via configuration rather than code. This paper provides a detailed system architecture for the AI-assisted pipeline. Rigorous evaluation on benchmarks demonstrates superior modularization (higher cohesion, lower inter-service coupling) compared to baselines. A case study on a large- scale e-commerce platform shows major gains in time-to-market and agility: policy changes once requiring full development cycles are now deployed within hours. This study addresses key non-functional needs such as security (consistent authentication, API gateway policies), data consistency (saga patterns, careful partitioning), and the vital role of human expertise through a human-in-the-loop paradigm. The result is a repeatable, low-risk pathway for modernization that combines advanced AI-driven analysis with configuration-centric design to achieve both structural and business agility. © 2026 IEEE.},
