	abstract = {Aiming at the specific problems of poor architectural scalability, low data processing efficiency, and insufficient system stability of the State Grid cloud collection open source components for high-reliability environments, this paper proposes an architectural optimization method based on microservice architecture and distributed processing technology. This method aims to improve the reliability, scalability, and processing efficiency of the system through modular design, service decoupling, and data parallel processing. This paper transforms the existing open source components into microservices, splits each functional module into independent microservices, realizes loose coupling between services, and improves the scalability and maintainability of the system. Apache Kafka is introduced as the middleware for data collection and processing to achieve asynchronous data processing and load balancing, and improve data processing efficiency. At the same time, Redis and Cassandra are used to store and manage the collected data to ensure high availability and consistency of the data. In addition, service fuse and degradation mechanisms, as well as comprehensive monitoring and logging, are implemented to enhance the stability and observability of components. After the architecture optimization, the performance of the State Grid cloud collection open source components has been improved. The data throughput has increased from an average of 495.92 KB/s before optimization to 702.8 KB/s, an increase of about 41.7 %. The maximum processing time has been reduced from 592 ms to 394 ms, and the minimum has been increased from 404 ms to 216 ms. The failure rate has been reduced from an average of 3.1872 % before optimization to 1.1764 %, a decrease of 63 %. These data fully demonstrate the effectiveness of architecture optimization in improving system performance and stability, and provide solid technical support for the digital transformation and intelligent upgrad