	url = {https://www.scopus.com/pages/publications/105044199357?origin=resultslist},
	abstract = {Service-based systems (SBS) depend on loosely coupled services that interact via standardized protocols, making testing challenging due to external dependencies. Mocking and service virtualization techniques have been proposed to simulate these dependencies and support automated testing, yet existing research lacks a unified synthesis of their approaches, effectiveness, and limitations. This systematic mapping study reviews 43 primary studies from IEEE Xplore, the ACM Digital Library, and Scopus on mock generation for SBS. We provide a structured taxonomy of mocking techniques and classify techniques into modeling and formal specification, contract-based testing, execution-based simulation, test generation, machine learning, and instrumentation. Our findings show that contract and specification-based methods constitute the majority of the papers we have examined, while AI-driven mock generation remains a sparsely-explored topic, tool support for scalable service virtualization is almost non-existent, and benchmark datasets are missing. We also identify cross-cutting aspects addressed by existing studies, including state management, service virtualization, behavior consistency, and reusability. These aspects highlight the challenges of maintaining realistic, reusable, and cost-effective mocks in distributed and evolving service-based systems. Evaluation practices rely mostly on laboratory experiments and prototype demonstrations, with limited industrial validation. Key challenges include scalability, behavioral fidelity, and maintainability, with emerging trends toward dynamic and AI-assisted mock generation. © 2026 Copyright held by the owner/author(s).},
