	abstract = {The spontaneous usage of cloud-native architecture has changed the way enterprises design, deploy, and scale software systems. Microservices have become the new reality of architecture every so-called style—is modular, scalable, and resolute but leads to new rules of operation due to their distributed character. The paper introduces the end-to-end DevOps model, which combines Cloud-Native Platform Engineering and GitOps practices and The Infrastructure as code (IaC) principles. It uses an approach that focuses on automation, reproducibility, and standardization of handling complex microservices deployments on multiplex environments. Utilizing the GitOps workflow to develop delivery processes and the use of IaC to provision infrastructure enables the platform engineering units to simplify the process of development pipelines, minimize the issue of configuration drift, and gain observability. The practical case study proves that deployment frequency, time to recover, and operational consistency have been significantly improved. Nonetheless, it has drawbacks such as high learning curve by development teams, possible toolchain fragmentation and governance issues in highly regulated industries. The future directions are the integration of AI-based observability, policy-as-code that would enable the automation of compliance and a closer interaction of platform engineering with FinOps practices to strike a balance between cost and performance in large-scale cloud-native ecosystems. © The Author(s), under exclusive license to Springer Nature Switzerland AG 2026.},
