	abstract = {The large-scale deployment of photovoltaic installations in remote locations underscores the need for effective monitoring systems. In a context where solar plants embed heterogeneous equipment, monitoring tools should be able to integrate with the installation regardless of their communication protocols. One of the major challenges is to ensure interoperability with equipment while guaranteeing effective big data management. The present work aims to propose a scalable and interoperable architecture for monitoring remote photovoltaic plants, capable of processing high-frequency data and managing operation and maintenance strategies. The proposed solution includes distributed data streaming, computation of performance indicators in accordance with the IEC 61724-1 standard, a modular anomaly detection pipeline and a real-time notification system. The modular microservice architecture also integrates edge devices for local data acquisition and processing. A quantitative data study was conducted to evaluate the system's performance. The results show a stable latency with an average of 3.4 s, with data completeness of 100% and 99.57% consistency. These findings highlight the ability of the architecture to monitor large-scale remote photovoltaic plants while ensuring flexibility required for the future integration of advanced analyses. © 2026 The Authors. Publishing services by Elsevier B.V. on behalf of KeAi Communications Co. Ltd. This is an open access article under the CC BY-NC-ND license. http://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-nc-nd/4.0/},
