	doi = {10.1109/BigData66926.2025.11400807},
	url = {https://www.scopus.com/pages/publications/105037833901?origin=resultslist},
	abstract = {Accurate forecasting of CPU and memory utilization is critical for proactive autoscaling and resource optimization in modern containerized cloud environments. However, the volatile dynamics of microservices, driven by fluctuating traffic and multi-tenant interference, introduce nonlinear and nonstationary behavior that challenges traditional predictors. While deep learning models such as LSTMs and Transformers have shown progress, they often lack robustness and interpretability, making it difficult to reason about their impact on quality-ofservice guarantees. To address these issues, this study proposes a novel NeuroSymbolic Ensemble Architecture (NSEA), which integrates a Neural Temporal Predictor for capturing usage patterns with a Symbolic Knowledge Engine that encodes operational policies and capacity constraints. An Adaptive Ensemble Fusion Layer dynamically combines these outputs to minimize prediction error while maintaining operational consistency. Extensive evaluation on the Alibaba Cluster Trace and Google Borg workload datasets demonstrates that NSEA achieves up to 28 % lower Mean Absolute Error compared to state-of-the-art baselines. By enabling more accurate and interpretable forecasts, NSEA supports improved adherence to Service Level Agreements (SLAs) in predictive autoscaling. These results validate NSEA as an explainable AI framework for predictive cloud orchestration, advancing neuro-symbolic intelligence toward more sustainable and autonomous resource management. © 2025 IEEE.},
