	abstract = {Good asynchronous communication and microservice coordination are the keys to achieving today's distributed systems fault tolerance and scalability. Decoupling services as asynchronous passing of messages leads to reduced latency, better throughput, and greater fault tolerance due to Event-Driven Architecture (EDA). Asynchronous communication flow best practices of scalable microsystem systems utilizing EDA models are presented here. It examines to what extent event sourcing, message queues, and broker-based systems can mitigate bottlenecks of synchronous inter-service communication. The study adopts a mixed-method research design that includes architectural simulation and performance benchmarking through throughput, latency, and resource usage. Through the strict experimentation and assessment, findings show the improved scalability of event-based systems when subjected to varied workloads. Findings confirm that EDA-based coordination does have a measurable impact on performance via decoupling coupling and real-time response. The experiment also recognizes issues in EDA deployment, including event schema design and data consistency. The solution plan aims at service boundary modularization, resilience, and efficiency at operation levels. The article concludes by implementing scaled microservices deployment best practices through architecting asynchronous workflows. These are used to direct cloud-native service-oriented software development practice and theoretical models of cloud-native service-oriented architecture.  © 2025 IEEE.},
