	abstract = {The ever-changing pace of web technology is a challenge for developers and quality assurance engineers to overcome: maintaining functional consistency and visual compatibility across a splintered browser landscape. Current web automation tools are highly effective at testing application functionality but are largely oblivious to underlying cross-browser compatibility problems, especially with respect to modern CSS and HTML capabilities. The proposed work presents BaseScript, a new, baseline-aware scripting language developed to fill this gap. BaseScript offers a high-level, human-readable YAML language that compiles into executable JavaScript for popular automation tools such as Puppeteer, Playwright, and Selenium. The key innovation of BaseScript is the incorporation of "Baseline data", a full database of web feature support, to support a unique 'baseline scan' command. This command enables dynamic, real-time analysis of a web page's DOM to identify and report on the use of non-baseline features during an automated test execution. The entire system is provided through a modern, web-based IDE with a live VNC browser preview, screenshot support, and a real-time terminal for instant feedback. © 2026 IEEE.},
