	abstract = {Distributed cloud commerce services increasingly make use of microservice architectures across multiple deployments and execution environments. In such systems, authentication context is often set at the platform edge and is implicitly trusted by downstream services, which can enable uncontrolled trust propagation, token replay, lateral movement, and delayed revocation under partial failure. This paper presents a formal threat model and a set of security invariants for decentralized authentication in distributed cloud commerce platforms. This is modeled as a region-spanning servicedependency graph in which each service validates cryptographically bound authentication context without dependence on centralized session state. The model is an adversary with attack on compromised services, token replay, cross-region misuse, and replication delay. This model is constructed from four security invariants: bounded trust propagation at each level of invocation depth, transitive privilege containment within token scope, cross-region replay resistance through region binding, and bounded revocation convergence under eventual consistency. These invariants are evaluated in the context of distributed cloud deployment, service mesh enforcement, token caching, and network partitioning. It lays the foundation for an elaborate set of decentralized authentication mechanisms that would exhibit predictable security behavior for large-scale cloud commerce systems. © 2026 IEEE.},
